membr:Project_Alhabyan a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Alhabyan_2611021636 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Valency, preposition governing, and phrasal verbs in Arabic and Semitic"@en .

membr:Project_Alhabyan_2611021636_F2611021636 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Provisional: Form I (faʾala) verbs of the CaCaCa vocalic pattern, denoting horizontal or vertical 'directed' motion, combine with the valency-increasing bound stem preposition [bi-] to denote a general notion of 'movement with' exhibited between two participants in the event. The same construction expanded into the more semantically specific \"bring with\" notion, whereby the first paritipant is more agentive, and the second assuming a more patient-like/passive character. From this latter semantics developed more lexically specified combinations, whereby the V+P combinations exhibit further unitary and noncompositional meanings, unpredictable from the elements of the construction. These \"idiomatic constructions\" are conditioned by the meaning of the verbal element, but also by the semantic features of the prepositional complement." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:araCla_ComitativeConstruction .

membr:Project_Badawi a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Badawi_2611021640 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Corpus-based measures of constructionhood"@en .

membr:Project_Badawi_2611021640_F2611021640 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label """1. The object slot of earn is strongly restricted lexically and semantically, while the subject slot is open. Against a whole-corpus baseline, the strongest object collexemes are living (G² = 3,130), money (1,799), wage, reputation, nickname, keep, respect and profit.\r
2. Semantically, money objects occur 8.3 times more often with earn than with verbs in general (10.7 times at the level of the money-and-business field).\r
3. Semantically, money objects occur 8.3 times more often with earn than with verbs in general (10.7 times at the level of the money-and-business field).\r
4. The money reading is preferred with I (G² = 43.9; where G² is the log-likelihood), you (G² = 19.3) and she (G² = 10.1), not with he, they, we or it.\r
Overall, the construction is  partially schematic: open in its subject, partially lexicalised in its object, with earn a living as its most strongly associated sub-pattern.\r
""" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engLME_EarnConstruction .

membr:Project_Bayer a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Bayer_2611042245,
        membr:Project_Bayer_2611042301,
        membr:Project_Bayer_2611042311 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Differential argument marking in Arabic"@en .

membr:Project_Bayer_2611042245_F2611042245 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The prepositional dative construction is used for ditransitive verbs if the recipient is expressed by a full NP, while the theme is pronominal or if both arguments are pronominal. The ditransitive construction cannot be used in these cases. In other constellations the constructions alternate." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:arzCai_Prepositionaldativeconstruction .

membr:Project_Bayer_2611042301_F2611042301 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The ditransitive construction alternates with the prepositional dative construction and the dative construction if the recipient is nominal or pronominal and the theme is expressed by a full NP." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:arzCai_Ditransitiveconstruction .

membr:Project_Bayer_2611042311_F2611042311 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The dative construction alternates with the prepositional dative construction and the ditransitive construction if the recipient is nominal or pronominal and the theme is expressed by a full NP." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:arzCai_Dativeconstruction .

membr:Project_BenitoFernandez a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_BenitoFernandez_2610301550 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Spanish quotative constructions in oral narratives — an Interactional Construction Grammar approach"@en .

membr:Project_BenitoFernandez_2610301550_F2610301550 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:spa_Decirquotativeconstruction .

membr:Project_Blake a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Blake"@en .

membr:Project_Boos a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Boos_2611051416,
        membr:Project_Boos_2611051450 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Entrenchment meets literacy: How the development of reading ability affects child and adult language processing"@en .

membr:Project_Boos_2611051416_F2611051416 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Research shows that subject relative clauses are typically more easily and faster processed than object relative clauses in right-branching languages like Spanish. Even in subject relatives where two NPs semantically compete for subject interpretation, speakers have no problem interpreting the syntactic structure of the clause. Conversely, interpretation of object relatives pose more problems to native speakers." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:spa_Subjectrelativeclause .

membr:Project_Boos_2611051450_F2611051450 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Research shows that object relative clauses are typically more difficult to process than subject relative clauses in right-branching languages like Spanish. In spoken language, object relatives follow a fairly stereotypical pattern: the second NP (Element 5) typically realised as a pronoun and the head NP (Element 1), i.e. the object of the relative clause, is usually an inanimate object. These factors can facilitate object relative clause processing. Object relatives, however, remain difficult to interpret when the two noun phrases semantically compete for subject/object interpretation. " ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:spa_Objectrelativeclause .

membr:Project_DelaGarza a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project De la Garza"@en .

<https://bdlweb.phil.uni-erlangen.de/RCxn/Abox/membr#Project_Dell'Erba> a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Pseudo-coordination with motion verbs in the Romance Languages: a CxG-Perspective"@en .

membr:Project_FernandezSantos a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_FernandezSantos_241125 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Artificial language learning as a window to the early entrenchment of constructions"@en .

membr:Project_FernandezSantos_241125 a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_FernandezSantos_241125_F250218 ;
    rsrch:projectName "The relative role of verb position and additional morphological markers in comprehension" .

membr:Project_FernandezSantos_241125_F250218 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The late disambiguation of verb-medial plain-variant (no a + article) object relative can lead to shallow processing. This in combination with high phonetic similarity with a canonical SR could lead to predictive processing overriding perceptual information (at the point of the last NP an object is expected so it’s more likely to hear \"al\" rather than \"el\"). In more general terms, expectations of canonical linguistic input in locally ambiguous structures can override acoustic perception." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:spaSpoPen_Potentiallyambiguousobjectrelativeclause .

membr:Project_Fokashchuk a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Fokashchuk_241210 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Functions and cognitive semantics of prepositions in complex constructions"@en .

membr:Project_Fokashchuk_241210 a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Fokashchuk_241210_F250218 ;
    rsrch:projectName "How are German NP1 + auf/an + NP2 constructions expressed in English, Polish, and Ukrainian, and to what extent do these languages employ spatial prepositions to convey similar functions and meanings? What are the cross-linguistic similarities and differences in form and meaning of these constructions?" .

membr:Project_Fokashchuk_241210_F250218 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label """(1) Most constructions across languages do not employ the same prepositions in terms of their spatial meaning, which challenges the assumption of uniform metaphorical and/or metonymic extensions. This indicates that when expressing similar scenes or relations between entities, different languages focus on distinct aspects of the relationship, or activate different "active zones" (Langacker 1991).\r\r
(2) There are some cross-linguistic correspondences when auf and an are used spatially, as auf/an are often translated as "on" in the other 3 languages. However, in abstract meanings, the semantic network diverges significantly: auf sometimes retains a connection to spatial "on", while an does not.\r\r
(3) Another parallelism: (a) an often corresponds to "of" in English and to the absence of a preposition but the object in genitive case in Polish and Ukrainian in NP1 + an + NP2 constructions. (b) Synonyms in English, Polish, and Ukrainian tend to govern the same prepositions, whereas this pattern is much less consistent in German.""" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:deuStdHigh_NP1PLUSaufPLUSNP2ANDNP1PLUSanPLUSNP2constructions,
        cx:deu_Angriffauf,
        cx:deu_Attentatauf,
        cx:deu_Ausblickauf,
        cx:deu_Hassauf,
        cx:deu_Hinweisauf,
        cx:deu_Konzentrationauf,
        cx:deu_Satireauf,
        cx:deu_Schussauf,
        cx:deu_UEberfallauf,
        cx:deu_UEbergriffauf,
        cx:deu_XanY,
        cx:deu_XaufY,
        cx:engLME_assassinationattempton,
        cx:engLME_assaulton,
        cx:engLME_attackonupon,
        cx:engLME_concentrationon,
        cx:engLME_hatredtowards,
        cx:engLME_satireon,
        cx:engLME_shotatto,
        cx:engLME_tiponreferenceto,
        cx:engLME_viewon,
        cx:pol_ataknaPLUSACC,
        cx:pol_koncentracjanaPLUSLOC,
        cx:pol_napadnaPLUSACC,
        cx:pol_nienawishchdoPLUSGEN,
        cx:pol_satyranaPLUSACC,
        cx:pol_strzawwPLUSACC,
        cx:pol_widoknaPLUSACC,
        cx:pol_wskazoowkanaPLUSACCodniesieniadoPLUSGEN,
        cx:pol_zamachnaPLUSACC,
        cx:ukr_KontsentratsiianaPLUSLOC,
        cx:ukr_NabihNalitNapadnaPLUSACC,
        cx:ukr_NapadnaPLUSACC,
        cx:ukr_NenavystdoPLUSGEN,
        cx:ukr_PostrilvPLUSACC,
        cx:ukr_SatyranaPLUSACC,
        cx:ukr_VkazivkanaPLUSACC,
        cx:ukr_VydnaPLUSACC,
        cx:ukr_ZamakhnaPLUSACC .

membr:Project_Fuehrer a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Fuehrer_F2512191334,
        membr:Project_Fuehrer_F2512191344,
        membr:Project_Fuehrer_F2512191440 ;
    rsrch:projectName "German verbs with particles or prefixes in language change: Form, meaning, and syntax"@en .

membr:Project_Fuehrer_F2512191334 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label """Subject is mostly animate (~85%), and sometimes inanimate (~15%).\r\r
Accusative object can fluctuate between a locality and things/persons, thus altering the interpretation of the construct.\r\r
Intensifying function occurs when there's already a directional element present in the base verb.""" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:gmh12C_PräfixverbkonstruktionHandlung .

membr:Project_Fuehrer_F2512191344 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "none" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:gmh12C_PräfixverbkonstruktionTätigkeit .

membr:Project_Fuehrer_F2512191440 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "directional information seemingly two times present, thus redundant" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:gmh12C_PräfixverbkonstruktionlokalistischeTätigkeit .

