membr:Project_Gromadsky a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Gromadsky_2611031634 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Constructions in communication"@en .

membr:Project_Gromadsky_2611031634_F2611031634 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "To signal that a referent does not match the canonical meaning of a word, interlocutors tend to use that word as a modifier of the general noun thing (occasionally type), as in a monster thing or a styrofoam thing." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engUkSpo_Xthingconstruction .

membr:Project_GroseHodge a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Grose-Hodge"@en .

membr:Project_Hutta a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Hutta_2610271233 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Hutta"@en .

membr:Project_Hutta_2610271233_F2610271233 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label """In dialects of German umlaut can be an obligatory element in a nominal plural construction, as well as an optional one. This means that depending on the specific regional variation, umlaut can serve as a singular element to form a nominal plural construction, it can be combined with a plural suffix to form a nominal plural construction, or the plural construction is only formed with a suffix. However,for 'Töchter' (dauchters) one element that forms plural is obligatory. \r
Aside from the number of elements that form the construction, there are a number of allomorphemic variants of each element, which are specific to the dialect or regional variation of German. """ ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:deu_Toechter .

membr:Project_Iabdounane a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Iabdounane_20260717 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Multimodal constructional space"@en .

membr:Project_Iabdounane_20260717 a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Iabdounane_F250218,
        membr:Project_Iabdounane_Fchefskiss,
        membr:Project_Iabdounane_Fmeso,
        membr:Project_Iabdounane_Fthiscloseto ;
    rsrch:projectName "How can multimodal phenomena be modelled in Construction Grammar?" .

membr:Project_Iabdounane_F250218 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The verbal form “this close to V-ing” and the close index finger - thumb gesture co-occur 93% of the time. The two forms are likely entrenched together as a single unit, thus making it one formal multimodal unit.\r The construction is multimodal." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:eng_ThisclosetoVingconstruction .

membr:Project_Iabdounane_Fchefskiss a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Chef’s Kiss exemplifies a gesture that functions as a construction in its own right, as it constitutes a form-meaning pairing. It can occupy specific slots within argument structure constructions, resulting in a multimodal construct where the gesture assumes a syntactic role." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:eng_ChefskissGesturalConstruction .

membr:Project_Iabdounane_Fmeso a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The meso construction is a semi-schematic multimodal construction: the verbal form is fixed, while the gestural component is a schematic slot. This slot accepts specific gesture types, primarily those indicating height or cutting motions. The meanings of these gestures are metaphorically extended to the domain of emotions conceptualized as a liquid." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:SmallQuantityGestureConstruction,
        cx:engUsSpo_Hadituptoherewith,
        cx:engUsSpo_Hadituptoherewithmesoconstruction,
        cx:engUsSpo_Hadituptoherewithphysicalheightgesture,
        cx:eng_PhysicalHeight,
        cx:eng_ThroatCutGesture .

membr:Project_Iabdounane_Fthiscloseto a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "This close to is an example of a multimodal construction in which the verbal form and the gesture are fixed and frequently co-occur. As such, it presents no significant challenges for modeling within Construction Grammar." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:eng_ThisclosetoVingconstruction .

membr:Project_Immertreu a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Immertreu_F2611031114 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Multimodal cognitive maps for cross-domain constructional networks"@en .

membr:Project_Immertreu_F2611031114 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "SR based cognitive maps differentiate unit from date constructions" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engLME_UnitConstruction .

membr:Project_Kashigin a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Kashigin_F2611032248 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Contrastive Construction Grammar: The interaction of argument-structure constructions and sentence-type constructions in english, Dutch and German"@en .

membr:Project_Kashigin_F2611032248 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:nld_DitransitiveConstructionOBJNP .

membr:Project_Kassler a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Kassler"@en .

membr:Project_Kenanidis a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Kenanidis"@en .

membr:Project_Kessler a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Kessler_F250218 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Chinese Mathematics"@en .

membr:Project_Kessler_F250218 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "There is remarkable stability in the use of the construction over a very long period of time" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:lzh_Dooperationwithoperand1onoperand2construction .

membr:Project_Khanoub a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Khanoub_2610141629,
        membr:Project_Khanoub_2610191625 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Competition and change in Modern English quantifier constructions"@en .

membr:Project_Khanoub_2610141629_F2610141629_A a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Finding 1: MANY appears to have remained relatively stable throughout the periods investigated. Its different patterns, such as many N, many of, many a, and elliptical many, continue to coexist, with relatively little evidence of major changes in its internal constructional organization. This is particularly interesting given that newer binominal quantifiers, such as a lot of, undergo changes over the same period." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:eng_many,
        cx:eng_manyPLUSNcountCx .

membr:Project_Khanoub_2610141629_F2610141629_B a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Finding 2: MANY and LOT overlap in the kinds of nouns they can quantify, but they do not behave in exactly the same way. Even in contexts where both are possible, their use is shaped by the N2 semantics and whether the quantified noun is modified. Rather than behaving as free synonyms, the binominal quantifier expression a lot of appears to have developed its own preferences alongside the well-established monomorphemic quantifier MANY." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:eng_many,
        cx:eng_manyPLUSNcountCx .

membr:Project_Khanoub_2610191625_F2610191625 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The construction shows a clear shift from predominantly lexical/literal uses towards more generalized quantification. While earlier historical uses often retain source meanings, later examples increasingly express well-established generalized quantity." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:eng_alotofPLUSNconstruction .

membr:Project_Kissane a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Form and meaning as factors in the identification and learning of constructional slots – English phrasal verbs and verb-preposition combinations"@en .

membr:Project_Kligge a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Representation and acquisition of agreement relations in a usage-based framework"@en .

membr:Project_Lee a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Lee_241202 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Lee"@en .

membr:Project_Lee_241202 a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Lee_241202_F250218 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Prepositional constructions - a corpuslinguistic study on collocation patterns and construction networks" .

membr:Project_Lee_241202_F250218 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Grammatically fixed with zero-realization, the \"vor-Augen construction\" displays a distinct collocational pattern closely tied to its meaning and contextual usage." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:deuWrSt20_sichNPADJADVvorAugenVP_MD .

membr:Project_Legoute a rsrch:Project ;
    membr:SubProject membr:Project_Legoute_241128 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Multifunctionality in Haitian Creole: New insights from a Construction Grammar perspective"@en .

membr:Project_Legoute_241128 a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Legoute_241128_F250218 ;
    rsrch:projectName "POU in Haitian Creole" .

membr:Project_Legoute_241128_F250218 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "POU provokes a NP to become a beneficiary" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:hat_POUbeneficiaryconstruction .

membr:Project_Makhanina a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Makhanina_F2611020737 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Representation and acquisition of idiomatic constructions in L1 and L2 learners"@en .

membr:Project_Makhanina_F2611020737 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "As an ergative language, Chechen uses ergative constructions in transitive clauses. Ergative construction gets constrained by progressive construction, i.e. in progressive the language employs neutral construction even in transitive clauses (encodes both subject and object with the same case - absolutive)" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:che_Ergativeconstruction .

membr:Project_Montevecchio a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Montevecchio_2611041733 ;
    rsrch:projectName "A constructional account of Prepositions and Prepositional Phrases in Early and Late Modern English"@en .

membr:Project_Montevecchio_2611041733_F2611041733 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Preposition phrases with metaphorical meanings using the preposition OVER in Legal % Administrative texts in Early Modern English tend to be mostly used with regards to the Restraint Image Schema." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engEME_Figurativecontrolconstruction .

membr:Project_Patel a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Patel_241128 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Corpus evidence for delineating constructions"@en .

membr:Project_Patel_241128 a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Patel_241128_F2608271115 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Identifying semantic sub-constructions of the V-ing pattern" .

membr:Project_Patel_241128_F2608271115 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "[provisional] In addition to demonstrating the usefulness of the QuACC analysis tool, the case study has shown ways in which measures of productivity, t-SNE plots of slot filler embeddings, and different kinds of collexeme analysis can help identify which sub-patterns are likely construction candidates. In particular, it has demonstrated how quantitative analyses can determine which slot restrictions are relevant for delineating a candidate, at which level(s) of schematicity they can be posited, and how restrictions on different slots co-vary." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:comePLUSnoisemannerofmovementVing .

membr:Project_Petrenko a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Petrenko_F2603061532,
        membr:Project_Petrenko_F2611031710 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Comparing constructions cross-linguistically — Connecting constructicons"@en .

membr:Project_Petrenko_F2603061532 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Comparative concepts can partially be applied to CASA Constructicon." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engLME_AdjectivalwithPARTNERConstruction,
        cx:engLME_AdjectiveConstruction,
        cx:engLME_ComparativeConstruction,
        cx:engLME_GeneralPostmodifierofAdjectiveConstruction,
        cx:engLME_MoreThanComparisonConstructiontype1,
        cx:engLME_MoreThanComparisonConstructiontype2,
        cx:engLME_MoreThanComparisonConstructiontype3,
        cx:engLME_MostLeastConstruction,
        cx:engLME_PremodifierofAdjectiveConstruction,
        cx:engLME_SuperlativeConstruction .

membr:Project_Petrenko_F2611031710 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Comparative concepts van partially be applied to the Russian Constructicon." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:rus_AdjNomCopNPNom .

membr:Project_Prela a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Prela"@en .

membr:Project_Ramezani a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Ramezani_250214_F250218 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Representation and processing of constructions in the brain"@en .

membr:Project_Ramezani_250214_F250218 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "So far we have results from LLM analysis that can significantly recognize and differentiate between argument structure constructions." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engLME_Argumentstructureconstruction,
        cx:engLME_Ditransitiveconstruction,
        cx:engLME_Resultativeconstruction,
        cx:engLME_Transitiveconstruction,
        cx:eng_CauseMotionconstruction .

membr:Project_Rastegar a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Representation and acquisition of idiomatic constructions in L1 and L2 learners"@en .

membr:Project_Rohwedder a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Rohwedder_2611021659 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Rohwedder"@en .

membr:Project_Rohwedder_2611021659_F2611021659 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The quotative conditional \"Wenn Sie sagen X dann Y\" schema that has been analysed by Buck & Schopf (2021) as part of an animated speech pattern that can help simulate the argumentation towards a medical decision can indeed serve different (but ultimately related) purposes in doctor-patient interactions. It is usually used by the doctor to yield authority to the patient and simultaneously clarify the implications of the patient's perceived stance from the medical point of view. Depending on how the quote relates to previous sequences, the whole utterance may either target epistemic authority, usually being of a more reflective and evaluative character, or deontic authority, with a more forward-oriented character, highlighting and possibly challenging the patient's perceived stance." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:deuSpo_QuotativeconditionalWennSiesagenXdannY .

membr:Project_Schmechel a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Schmechel_2610160927 ;
    rsrch:projectName "A constructional approach to prepositions and prepositional phrases in Old English"@en .

membr:Project_Schmechel_2610160927_F2610160927 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Through distinctive collexeme analysis, it was possible to determine that a considerable number of verbs and nouns that are linked to meanings which express a change of state are attracted to the preposition 'to'. These verbs and nouns were among the 10 most strongly attracted words." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:ang_Changeofstateconstruction .

membr:Project_Senger a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Senger_2610301632 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Individual language change across the lifespan"@en .

membr:Project_Senger_2610301632_F2610301632 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "(provisional) In American English, the frequency of some of the so-called “GenX so” constructions seem to be declining continuously (Stange 2023). The findings of the project are expected to show how the linguistic behaviour of an individual speaker develops over time in relation to this observed community-level change, and to provide more detailed insights into patterns of age-grading, lifespan change, and retrograde change in the linguistic individual." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engUsSpo_XissoNP .

membr:Project_Stampfer a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:projectName "Semantically related argument structures in the history of English"@en .

membr:Project_Trombetta a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Trombetta_F250218 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Constructions beyond the sentence: text-structuring in (esp.) sixteenth-century historiographical texts"@en .

membr:Project_Trombetta_F250218 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The text reveals various types of text-level constructions, i.e., constructions capable of structuring and organizing the text. These structures, while formally appearing as sentences or phrases, operate at the textual level." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:itaStoria_RelativeTopicShiftConstruction .

membr:Project_Tzimas a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Tzimas_F2611021411 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Subject-inversion throughout Early Modern English: changing relations in individual and communal constructions"@en .

membr:Project_Tzimas_F2611021411 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The FVI construction follows an XVS word order where X is an Adverb Phrase, a Prepositional Phrase, an Adjective Phrase or a Noun Phrase. V can be any full verb, excluding be and have when they are in auxiliary form, and modal verbs such as must, can, should etc. S is any noun phrase in subject position. Findings so far indicate that this construction splits in two different sub-constructions, Locative and Temporal FVI, according to the semantics of X. These sub constructions have separate formal and pragmatic preferences and roles." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engEME_TheFVIconstruction .

membr:Project_Weigelt a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Weigelt_F2610211337 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Project Weigelt"@en .

membr:Project_Weigelt_F2610211337 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Attaching {los} to a base verb marks ingressive aspect in Present Day German. {los} has marked ingressive aspect in base verbs since Early New High German. The construction further marks agentiveness, and requires an AGENS as argument role. The Argument Structure is monovalent/intransitive." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:deuFNG_losingressiveconstruction .

membr:Project_Winckel a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Winckel_F2511281100 ;
    rsrch:hasResearchQuestion membr:Project_Winckel_2601131454 ;
    rsrch:projectName "Building a Research Constructicon"@en .

membr:Project_Winckel_2601131454_F2601131454 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "The relational database for CASA entries can be translated into the RDF format of the Research Constructicon." ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engLME_AttributeConstructionAttrAdj,
        cx:eng_GenitiveNPConstruction,
        cx:eng_ditransitive_construction_obj_np,
        cx:lat_Adjective .

membr:Project_Winckel_F2511281100 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label "Implement a lexical item in the research constructicon" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:_coolconstruction,
        cx:engInf_Coolexclamativeconstruction,
        cx:eng_AdjectiveAttributiveconstruction,
        cx:eng_AdjectivePhrase,
        cx:eng_AdjectivePredicativeconstruction,
        cx:eng_Keepacoolheadconstruction,
        cx:eng_coolconstruction .

